ur view to be exactly the same  so facts you could take your 4 minutes  to set your stall out thank you and I  think a useful distinction that you've  set out David is between moral  psychology and ethics so what do we  actually do in practice and what do we  think we should do and I would agree  that what we actually do in practice is  we behave and we feel model impulses  that are very tribal because we evolved  in tribes and that was something that  was really essential for our survival I  think whether we should accept that is a  different question and I don't think we  should there are two things that I would  say about that firstly when we look back  over human moral progress we could see  it as a sort of expanding circle of  individuals that we believe fall within  circle of compassion  so from originally perhaps just the  family to cities to States to nations to  different races and all of humanity and  of course hopefully not stopping there  and in terms of some of the most  shameful things that humans have done to  one another I think we can attribute  some of those great atrocities to a  failure to continue to expand our moral  circle so in the fifth century BC it was  perfectly acceptable for Greek  city-states that would be the limits of  your moral sphere of concern and Peter  Singer in his book the expanding circle  refers to a tombstone of a warrior and  on the same tombstone totally without  irony it says remember his name this  warrior killed seven men and speared  their bodies into seven parts he goes  down loved by all never having hurt a  man on the face of the earth and because  you're so completely outside the sphere  of concern and we've seen this failure  too  think beyond tribalism in many places in  terms of ongoing racism sexism  discrimination entire groups have been  marginalized for reasons that made sense  within the conventional tribal wisdom of  the time but which now looking back are  abhorrent and are deeply shameful and I  think 